#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138169 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138413 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d9375c5-8905-5a25-8585-47a1861238c0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Variation Equation Given One Data Point"}] :question/id #uuid "8a12b2fd-6361-59ef-8378-6c900a1ab47b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14089" :question/problem "In an inverse variation, $y=5$ when $x=8.$ Find an equation that relates $x$ and $y.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nNext, we use the data $y=5$ when $x=8$ to find $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =\\frac{k}{8} \\\\ 5\\cdot 8 & =k \\\\ 40 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our inverse variation equation is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{40}{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138170 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138414 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e239c25-66f9-5ea7-ba3c-5599966cd89b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Second Data Point Given Another Data Point"}] :question/id #uuid "a2d2a2a1-da7b-5106-8347-3a4f71ddd619" :question/math-academy-id "e-14090" :question/problem "If $y$ varies inversely with $x$ and $y=15$ when $x=\\dfrac{3}{5}$, find the value of $y$ when $x=3.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $y=15$ when $x=\\dfrac{3}{5}.$ We substitute this data into the equation to find $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & =\\frac{k}{(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\ 15\\cdot (\\frac{3}{5}) & =k \\\\ \\frac{45}{5} & =k \\\\ 9 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the inverse variation is given by \n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{9}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the value of $x=3$ into this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{9}{3} = 3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138172 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138415 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c4d2228-2456-536c-87f5-c05f03ac1532" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Table Represents an Inverse Variation"}] :question/id #uuid "80333f8b-9aff-5f31-8301-a8708d531a22" :question/math-academy-id "e-14092" :question/problem "| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $0.5$ | $16$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ |\n| $4$ | $2$ |\n\nConsider the table above. Which of the following statements is true?\n\nI. $y$ is inversely proportional to $x$ with constant of variation $k=8.$\n\nII. $y$ is inversely proportional to $x$ with constant of variation $k=16.$\n\nIII. $y$ is not inversely proportional to $x.$" :question/worked-solution "If $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ then we must have\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{k}{x},\n$$\n\nfor some constant $k.$ Note that we can rewrite the above equation as follows.\n\n$$\nx \\cdot y = k\n$$\n\nSo, we will compute $x\\cdot y = k$ for each row in the table. If we get the same value of $k$ for each row, then we can conclude that $y$ is inversely proportional to $x.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $x\\cdot y = k$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $0.5$ | $16$ | $0.5\\cdot 16=8$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $2\\cdot 4=8$ |\n| $4$ | $2$ | $4\\cdot 2=8$ |\n\nThe results are all the same: in each row, we have $k=8.$ Therefore, $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ and the constant of variation is $k=8.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138173 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138416 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "482e3ce6-1938-5bbe-a0d3-2c44213c7746" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Variation Equation Given a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "9c285374-0465-52ed-a283-c469e123d167" :question/math-academy-id "e-14094" :question/problem "![](images/02/02cf003d5046e2c6486cc11df95d8fae60bf9981ec8f0934621f18b4fa509153.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the relation between $x$ and $y.$ Given that $y$ varies inversely with $x$, find the inverse variation equation between $x$ and $y.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nIn the graph, we notice a point with $x=5$ and $y=5.$ We substitute this data into the equation to find $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =\\frac{k}{5} \\\\ 5\\cdot 5 & =k \\\\ 25 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse variation equation is given by\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{25}{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498567 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138413 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d9375c5-8905-5a25-8585-47a1861238c0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Variation Equation Given One Data Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15863a2c-d5b0-54e0-9a14-d6c06514bc21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{36}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497849} {:answer/id #uuid "8059bbfc-ab51-50e6-874d-74c86946ae6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{9}{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498099} {:answer/id #uuid "858d3684-4ea3-50e1-8d23-e40d14e3a2d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{4}{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498106} {:answer/id #uuid "be6bfc4b-81a4-5001-9bba-93c029505c4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{36x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498222} {:answer/id #uuid "c8c77c11-e5ee-577a-a88c-c9593dbf5295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{18}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15863a2c-d5b0-54e0-9a14-d6c06514bc21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{36}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497849} :answer-field/id #uuid "27034242-20eb-5a94-9bd8-e62a5e62514f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e4a7397-a5bb-5138-9c78-655eebc20cf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-115474" :question/problem "In an inverse variation, $y=9$ when $x=-4.$ Find an equation that relates $x$ and $y.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nNext, we use the given data $y=9$ when $x=-4$ to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & =\\frac{k}{(-4)} \\\\ 9\\cdot (-4) & =k \\\\ -36 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our inverse variation equation is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{36}{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498521 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138413 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d9375c5-8905-5a25-8585-47a1861238c0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Variation Equation Given One Data Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28897761-1f3a-503d-bb0e-e86918aefadc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{8}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497883} {:answer/id #uuid "2bd5b492-9cdd-5ee6-a39b-98a362d52f69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497890} {:answer/id #uuid "5c1cf9d2-f464-54c6-a549-4365a70f5266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498020} {:answer/id #uuid "8ace9209-ef2b-59e6-9e93-3991e1c4ebe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498119} {:answer/id #uuid "be86a5c5-c0fb-5c1b-a3ae-5335c31347f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be86a5c5-c0fb-5c1b-a3ae-5335c31347f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498223} :answer-field/id #uuid "400979f2-2882-5327-9e82-7bf605061219" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3fc4c816-ede7-5d8a-b5e9-ddf8344d7ae4" :question/math-academy-id "q-115478" :question/problem "In an inverse variation, $y=\\frac{1}{4}$ when $x=2.$ Find an equation that relates $x$ and $y.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nNext, we use the given data $y=\\frac{1}{4}$ when $x=2$ to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{k}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 2 & =k \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our inverse variation equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498497 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138414 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e239c25-66f9-5ea7-ba3c-5599966cd89b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Second Data Point Given Another Data Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45e6265d-627c-5bfe-b4a4-c9064cb498a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497959} {:answer/id #uuid "460eaa64-2244-54be-b725-8a2f2bf21ee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497960} {:answer/id #uuid "576b3890-6abd-55de-92db-73d43f720e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498006} {:answer/id #uuid "7fd59b4d-9677-5eda-a75f-2e81639dd1ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498097} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf3078c-b7b8-5e5f-a2a5-c5b18a6edb52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "576b3890-6abd-55de-92db-73d43f720e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498006} :answer-field/id #uuid "6accb65d-487d-5e4c-9f58-e9f7512e092a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498410}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "157c2ce3-5327-5267-a129-7f9aebf9b49f" :question/math-academy-id "q-115560" :question/problem "If $y$ varies inversely with $x$ and $y=3$ when $x=\\frac{5}{3},$ find the value of $y$ when $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $y=3$ when $x=\\frac{5}{3}.$ We substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =\\frac{k}{(\\frac{5}{3})} \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} & =k \\\\ \\frac{15}{3} & =k \\\\ 5 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the inverse variation is given by \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the value of $x=2$ into this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138413 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d9375c5-8905-5a25-8585-47a1861238c0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Variation Equation Given One Data Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08f72752-b6a3-5fac-90f5-71764ab5509e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08f72752-b6a3-5fac-90f5-71764ab5509e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497819} :answer-field/id #uuid "15914c78-3443-5f37-8613-b5a6f0de9715" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "61e255c1-0021-502e-a47e-13b6e810d018" :question/math-academy-id "q-212099" :question/problem "In an inverse variation, $y=\\frac{3}{8}$ when $x=2.$ Find an equation that relates $x$ and $y.$\n\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nNext, we use the given data $y=\\frac{3}{8}$ when $x=2$ to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8} & =\\frac{k}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{8}\\cdot 2 & =k \\\\ \\frac{6}{8} & =k \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our inverse variation equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138751 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138413 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d9375c5-8905-5a25-8585-47a1861238c0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Variation Equation Given One Data Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f132f7e-60ad-5452-a2eb-ff4b5d68c418" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{42}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f132f7e-60ad-5452-a2eb-ff4b5d68c418" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{42}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497899} :answer-field/id #uuid "29d53d75-e2e6-5ac7-aa59-d2b2ebca63f7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ccf09da9-31a3-5a40-9ece-1b4f5083edcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-217121" :question/problem "In an inverse variation, $y=6$ when $x=-7.$ Find an equation that relates $x$ and $y.$\n\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nNext, we use the given data $y=6$ when $x=-7$ to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =\\frac{k}{(-7)} \\\\ 6\\cdot (-7) & =k \\\\ -42 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our inverse variation equation is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{42}{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498519 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138415 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c4d2228-2456-536c-87f5-c05f03ac1532" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Table Represents an Inverse Variation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd9afd47-8870-5dce-b868-e91c5ce20f62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "directly" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498219} {:answer/id #uuid "ec968006-3a4b-5c45-a0a5-1e2f616e7e7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inversely" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec968006-3a4b-5c45-a0a5-1e2f616e7e7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inversely" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498318} :answer-field/id #uuid "b08c00b7-2402-5844-b574-287c4bc50115" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498445} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e6d8f00-2030-5663-a9a7-4a2e4758e45b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497941} {:answer/id #uuid "90fd24b2-3e1e-56ea-94a3-a67bee566d7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498132} {:answer/id #uuid "9968f11b-6765-5930-93fd-d8242102c550" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498147} {:answer/id #uuid "d0002b8a-73e2-536b-8292-bbfbf4151fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0002b8a-73e2-536b-8292-bbfbf4151fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498271} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdfbbcef-bf26-5772-810b-7bd392bc9c98" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3da28d76-c23e-56b2-b98e-5a4dbf8f4efa" :question/math-academy-id "q-217253" :question/problem "| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $0.3$ | $20$ |\n| $0.6$ | $10$ |\n| $2$ | $3$ |\n\nGiven the table above, select the options that make the following statement true:\n\nThe variable $y$ is {{field-1}} proportional to $x,$ and the constant of variation is $k=$  {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "If $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ then we must have\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nfor some constant $k.$ Note that we can rewrite the above equation as follows.\n\n$$\nx\\cdot y=k\n$$\n\nSo, we will compute $x\\cdot y=k$ for each row in the table. If we get the same value of $k$ for each row, then we can conclude that $y$ is inversely proportional to $x.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $x\\cdot y=k$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $0.3$ | $20$ | $0.3\\cdot 20=6$ |\n| $0.6$ | $10$ | $0.6\\cdot 10=6$ |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $2\\cdot 3=6$ |\n\nThe results are all the same: in each row, we have $k=6.$ Therefore, we conclude:\n\nThe variable $y$ is $\\text{inversely}$ proportional  to $x,$ and the constant of variation is $k=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138415 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c4d2228-2456-536c-87f5-c05f03ac1532" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Table Represents an Inverse Variation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23a4774a-3eec-5fe8-a35b-7924a8f896be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497873} {:answer/id #uuid "39379ff7-d28b-54f8-b004-8168e94feeae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497926} {:answer/id #uuid "928fccb1-2b97-5443-90ad-c0afdbef9b28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498133} {:answer/id #uuid "ac3f8911-bb59-5625-bccc-cceb648ee7ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23a4774a-3eec-5fe8-a35b-7924a8f896be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497873} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cd7f47c-05e6-57af-902e-c3e2f8c4acce" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498366} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "063a32cc-64a8-5d9b-8bbb-778e4ce065c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "directly" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497811} {:answer/id #uuid "775f6874-f183-5625-8825-10261bb31e31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inversely" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "775f6874-f183-5625-8825-10261bb31e31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inversely" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498083} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dc4d23e-683f-5389-ade4-a228e326ad50" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2fadf069-0681-5a52-8bef-b8793387f31e" :question/math-academy-id "q-217254" :question/problem "| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $3.5$ |\n| $7$ | $2$ |\n| $14$ | $1$ |\n\nGiven the table above, select the options that make the following statement true:\n\nThe variable $y$ is {{field-1}} proportional to $x,$ and the constant of variation is $k=$  {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "If $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ then we must have\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nfor some constant $k.$ Note that we can rewrite the above equation as follows.\n\n$$\nx\\cdot y=k\n$$\n\nSo, we will compute $x\\cdot y=k$ for each row in the table. If we get the same value of $k$ for each row, then we can conclude that $y$ is inversely proportional to $x.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $x\\cdot y=k$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $3.5$ | $4\\cdot 3.5=14$ |\n| $7$ | $2$ | $7\\cdot 2=14$ |\n| $14$ | $1$ | $14\\cdot 1=14$ |\n\nThe results are all the same: in each row, we have $k=14.$ Therefore, we conclude:\n\nThe variable $y$ is $\\text{inversely}$ proportional  to $x,$ and the constant of variation is $k=14.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498534 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138416 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "482e3ce6-1938-5bbe-a0d3-2c44213c7746" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Variation Equation Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d46e461-53f4-5a4c-aa29-7447146a4f04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d46e461-53f4-5a4c-aa29-7447146a4f04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497938} :answer-field/id #uuid "8df595bc-00dd-5e1e-9875-a07a226991b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e4b31ea-beda-5fbb-b941-d610f8f1a932" :question/math-academy-id "q-218382" :question/problem "![](images/bd/bd4cccf280ca8bb71b8c54bafc79959431b78540a047dcb1120785e603abd249.png)\n\nThe graph above shows an inverse variation relationship between $x$ and $y.$ Express $y$ in terms of $x.$\n\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nIn the graph, we notice a point with $x=8$ and $y=\\frac{3}{2}.$ We substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & =\\frac{k}{8} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 8 & =k \\\\ 12 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse variation equation is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{12}{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498500 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138414 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e239c25-66f9-5ea7-ba3c-5599966cd89b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Second Data Point Given Another Data Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e41d7eb-d6b5-5b2c-946b-12009ab1f71f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498095} {:answer/id #uuid "9784723c-c70e-51dd-9e53-5d403867e4f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498143} {:answer/id #uuid "b24df18f-c0d7-55b0-a620-52ca08dd9606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498198} {:answer/id #uuid "b803ed2a-92df-50aa-86b4-a74a3d347a8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498209} {:answer/id #uuid "c5e978c6-725e-52b7-a7d5-e19dd3beea3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b24df18f-c0d7-55b0-a620-52ca08dd9606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498198} :answer-field/id #uuid "7738aed2-6969-583c-b1d2-159e3752029b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19618a93-f5c7-57b8-b5e4-4ba96b1ca9c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-51816" :question/problem "If $y$ varies inversely with $x$ and $y=10$ when $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ find the value of $y$ when $x=3.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $y=10$ when $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$ We substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =\\frac{k}{(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\ 10\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}) & =k \\\\ \\frac{30}{2} & =k \\\\ 15 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the inverse variation is given by \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{15}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the value of $x=3$ into this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{15}{3}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498536 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138416 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "482e3ce6-1938-5bbe-a0d3-2c44213c7746" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Variation Equation Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03509785-63f5-5445-8ab5-efc8af5db658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{6}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497805} {:answer/id #uuid "4d7ac2c9-6132-5f14-aa53-56106ae322aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497982} {:answer/id #uuid "7e8a8c85-4138-5aef-b7e9-c9d98c9335dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498096} {:answer/id #uuid "9c0a8ef5-b908-5ec0-873a-721c483aabe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498149} {:answer/id #uuid "a0d997a5-88f5-58af-9c73-ba0cf2bb6ba4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{9}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03509785-63f5-5445-8ab5-efc8af5db658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{6}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497805} :answer-field/id #uuid "26777947-b062-512b-9c23-c9ecd09ea93d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "53ba14a9-4fd5-5080-a5a5-86813a5ef68d" :question/math-academy-id "q-51895" :question/problem "![](images/38/383c7f4412b9c9b9017e8b93745191362ac2612cedc741b6aba6ee2bcfa13f21.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the relationship between $x$ and $y.$ Given that $y$ varies inversely with $x$, find the inverse variation equation between $x$ and $y.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{x}.\n$$\n\nIn the graph, we notice a point with $x=3$ and $y=2.$ We substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{k}{3} \\\\ 2\\cdot 3 & =k \\\\ 6 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse variation equation is given by\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{6}{x}.\n$$"}]}
